"07_dialogs.mst — the MODAL LOOP, on this port's terms (DD12).

 Dolphin runs a modal dialog by FORKING A GREEN UI PROCESS
 (`DialogView>>runModalLoop`: `Processor forkMainIfMain`, priorities, a
 Semaphore to wait on) — process machinery this port deliberately replaced
 with Dart isolates and does not intend to imitate. The user's standing rule
 decides what to do instead: 'we are running the dolphin MVP but we are not
 dolphin... it is the GUI behaviour we need.'

 THE GUI BEHAVIOUR of a modal is: the owner is disabled, the dialog answers
 input, everything else keeps painting, and control returns to the caller when
 the dialog closes. That is exactly a NESTED MESSAGE LOOP in the current
 process — which is also what a native Win32 DialogBox does, and which
 Dolphin itself offers as `runModalInProcessLoop`:

     self isModal: true.
     SessionManager current inputState loopWhile: [self isModal]

 So the compat surface is two small pieces: `InputState>>loopWhile:` over the
 provisional pump, and `runModalLoop` routed to the in-process variant.
 STACKED modals fall out for free — each `showModal` nests one more
 `loopWhile:`, and they unwind innermost-first, which is the only order a
 single-threaded pump can unwind in and the order the goal gate asserts."

InputState extend [
    "Pump until aBlock answers false. Sleeps when the queue is empty for the
     same reason `UiSession runFor:` does: a tight PeekMessage loop on an
     empty queue burns a core. Deferred actions drain inside `pump`, which is
     what lets a gate drive a modal it is itself blocked under: the action it
     posted BEFORE entering `showModal` runs from the nested loop."
    loopWhile: aBlock [
        [ aBlock value ] whileTrue: [
            UiSession pump.
            KernelLibrary sleepEx: 5 bAlertable: false ]
    ]
]

DialogView extend [
    "In-process IS this port's process model, so the fork-a-UI-process loop
     routes to Dolphin's own in-process variant rather than reimplementing
     it. `isModal:` false is set by the dialog's own close path, which is what
     ends the loop."
    runModalLoop [
        ^self runModalInProcessLoop
    ]
]

"── Semaphore: the fork handshake, degenerate in one process ────────────
 `DialogView>>initialize` creates one (`endModal := Semaphore new`) and the
 fork-based loop waits on it while `destroy` signals it from the OTHER
 process. With the modal loop running IN-PROCESS there is no other process
 and nothing ever waits: the loop ends because `destroy` set `isModal: false`,
 which is the condition `loopWhile:` tests.

 So this is a no-op pair, and that is the honest shape rather than an
 omission — `Semaphore` is otherwise an unbound global, so `initialize` would
 leave `endModal` nil and `[sem signal] postToMessageQueue` would fail inside
 the close path, contained, leaving a dialog that will not go away.

 NOT a general Semaphore. It answers exactly the two selectors this handshake
 uses. Anything needing real blocking semantics must not reach for this —
 this port's concurrency is Dart isolates (st/dolphin_compat/11_worker.mst)."
Object subclass: Semaphore [
    signal [ ^self ]
    wait [ ^self ]
    Semaphore class >> new [ ^self basicNew ]
]

"`postToMessageQueue` — Dolphin defers a block to the UI queue. This port has
 that exact mechanism already: `UiSession postAction:`, drained by the pump
 (and so by the nested modal pump too)."
BlockClosure extend [
    postToMessageQueue [ ^UiSession postAction: self ]
]

"── a dialog is an ORDINARY WINDOW here ─────────────────────────────────
 Dolphin creates a DialogView with `CreateDialogParamW` over a Win32 DIALOG
 RESOURCE TEMPLATE (`CreateDialog>>create:`: `lpTemplate: aDialogView
 templateId asResourceId`) plus a DLGPROC. This port ships no resource DLL and
 has no dialog procedure — `templateId` is nil, and the first probe died on
 `int has no method asResourceId` before a window ever existed.

 The GUI BEHAVIOUR of a modal needs none of that. A modal is: an owned popup
 with a caption, the owner disabled, a nested message loop, and control
 returning on close. Every one of those is already working here on the
 ORDINARY create path (`View>>creationFunction:dpi:` -> `CreateWindow`), which
 is exactly how a native Win32 app writes a modal that is not `DialogBox`.

 So creation is re-pointed and EVERYTHING ELSE in DialogView is Dolphin's
 untouched: `showModal`'s owner enable/disable and `ensure:` block, `answer`,
 `isModal`, the close path. `defaultWindowStyle` stays Dolphin's
 (13107200 = WS_POPUP | WS_CAPTION | WS_SYSMENU | DS_MODALFRAME-ish), so the
 window LOOKS like the dialog it is.

 RETIREMENT: if this port ever ships a resource DLL with real dialog
 templates, delete this override and Dolphin's own path resumes."
DialogView extend [
    creationFunction: aRectangle dpi: anInteger [
        ^CreateWindow
            rectangle: aRectangle
            dpi: anInteger
            styles: self creationStyles
            title: self windowTitle
    ]

    "`endDialog:` is EndDialog(), which only destroys a window DialogBox
     created — ours it does not, and Dolphin's `destroy` then deliberately
     skips `super destroy` on the modal path, leaving the window on screen
     forever. Here the dialog IS an ordinary window and there is no second
     process to hand destruction to, so it always destroys."
    destroy [
        self isModal: false.
        ^super destroy
    ]
]

"── MonitorFromPoint: a POINT passed BY VALUE ───────────────────────────
 `DisplayMonitor class >> nearestPoint:` calls
 `User32 monitorFromPoint: aPoint asParameter dwFlags: N`, and that prim does
 not exist on this floor — `MonitorFromPoint(POINT pt, DWORD flags)` takes an
 8-byte struct BY VALUE, which `genprims` refuses outright rather than guess
 an ABI (the same refusal that drops REFGUID and CURRENCY). Untranslated it
 was `_Type has no method monitorFromPoint_dwFlags_` INSIDE `showModal`, so
 the modal never opened.

 Answered here with `MonitorFromRect`, which takes a RECT by POINTER and is
 fully representable — a degenerate rect at the point is the documented
 equivalent, and both API calls share the same MONITOR_DEFAULTTO* flags. Not
 a guess at the by-value ABI: a different, exact API doing the same job.

 RETIREMENT: when the FFI floor learns small-struct-by-value (the x64 rule is
 that an 8-byte POD travels in one register), `genprims` emits the real
 `MonitorFromPoint` and this override goes."
UserLibrary extend [
    UserLibrary class >> monitorFromPoint: aPoint dwFlags: anInteger [
        | r |
        r := RECT newBuffer.
        r left: aPoint x; top: aPoint y; right: aPoint x + 1; bottom: aPoint y + 1.
        ^User32 monitorFromRect: r yourAddress dwFlags: anInteger
    ]
]

"── SharedLookupTable ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 Dolphin's thread-safe LookupTable, used where more than one green process
 might touch a cache. `DisplayMonitor class >> reset` builds its hMonitor ->
 monitor map with one, so an unbound global there meant `Instances` stayed nil
 and every window-positioning path — including `showModal`'s centring — died
 on `at:ifAbsentPut:` sent to nil, contained, so the modal never opened.

 A plain LookupTable here, and that is not a shortcut: this port's UI runs on
 ONE thread (the door's pump; concurrency is Dart isolates, which share no
 objects), so there is nothing for the lock to exclude. The name is kept
 because the corpus asks for it by name.

 RETIREMENT: if image-side objects ever become reachable from more than one
 OS thread, this needs real mutual exclusion — and so would a great deal
 else, which is why it is recorded rather than defended."
LookupTable subclass: SharedLookupTable [ ]
